  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Dangerously hot conditions continue.\n\nWith a series of high pressures above us and a large ridge in place \nover the Mid-Atlantic, dangerously hot conditions are to continue \ninto today and tomorrow (Saturday). Heat indices are likely to be \nover 105 degrees east of the Blue Ridge and above 110 degrees deep \nin the Piedmont. To reflect this, adjustments have been made to the \nHeat Headlines. For today, the Heat Advisories and Extreme Heat \nWarnings stay in place with an exception to Caswell and Rockingham \nCounties which have been upgraded to an Extreme Heat Warning. While \nthe upper level ridge starts to crack by Saturday, similar summer \nconditions are likely to persist and the heat headlines today apply \nto the same counties and timeframe tomorrow. Once again, the public \nis encouraged this holiday weekend to use extreme caution to prevent \nheat related illnesses. Drink plenty of water, limit time outdoors, \nand know the symptoms of heat exhaustion and heat stroke.\n\n\nKey Message 2: Severe weather possible this weekend followed by additional \nchances of showers next week.\n\nWhat sets the scorching heat days of today and Saturday from \nthe previous days is the chance of showers. Today there is a \nmodest chance of thunderstorms and showers in the region, \nparticularly along and west of the Blue Ridge. CAM guidance \nsuggests these storms will be pulse based and bring the risk of \ndamaging wind gusts. The lack of shear in the area will prevent \nany storm organization so most storms are not expected to live \nlong. That being said, storms today will still have to fight \nthrough heavy subsidence just to get going.\n\nSaturday's storm chances look more promising as the upper level \nridge starts to break down. On this day, the distribution of \nscattered storms looks to be more widespread as well as the damaging \nwind gust threat. As a result, the Storm Prediction Center has \nincreased in spatial coverage of a Slight Risk (Level 2/5) of severe \nweather for the region.\n\nFollowing the weekend storms, the ridge continues to degrade and \nwill open the door for additional disturbances to move through. A \nmid-level trough will slowly move east across the Great Lakes next \nweek, bringing an associated surface front towards the Mid-Atlantic \nand central CONUS. The front is not expected to move much which \ntranslates into multiple opportunities for showers and thunderstorms \nfor next week.",
